### Recommended next implementation stage — exactly one

**Name**: *Named Deployment Threading — retire the
`F_XHTTP_ENABLE`/`TELEMT_DOMAIN`/`TELEMT_PORT` positional chain*

**Goal**: Replace the positional/re-defaulted threading of these three
values (currently re-derived or re-defaulted independently at every
hop: `cli.sh` → `install.sh` → `nginx/config.sh`'s `$11`/`$12`/`$13` →
`variant_f.sh`/`variant_j.sh` → `api.sh`'s own re-derivation) with reads
from the already-designed, already-partially-consumed `Deployment`
model (`Deployment.capabilities` containing `"XHTTP"`;
`Deployment.telemt.domain`/`.port`) — the exact target shape
`docs/CORE_RUNTIME_CONTRACTS.md` §10 already specifies, verified this
session to still be accurate.

**Concrete files** (all independently confirmed this session to carry
one or more of the three values):
- `lib/panel/cli.sh` (source of the three values)
- `lib/panel/install.sh` (positional relay: `install.sh:170,221-223,
  284,304`)
- `lib/panel/nginx/config.sh` (positional consumption: `$11`, `$12`,
  `$13`)
- `lib/panel/nginx/variant_f.sh`, `lib/panel/nginx/variant_j.sh`
  (consumers, not re-defaulters)
- `lib/panel/api.sh` (independent re-derivation of `F_XHTTP_ENABLE`
  from `MODE` at `install.sh:304`'s call site, per §10's own citation)

**Responsibilities that change**: `cli.sh` still *collects* the three
raw values (no change to prompts/UX) but hands them to
`core_resolve_deployment()` instead of threading them positionally
through `install.sh`; every downstream consumer
(`nginx/config.sh`/`variant_f.sh`/`variant_j.sh`/`api.sh`) reads
`Deployment.capabilities`/`Deployment.telemt` instead of its own
positional argument or independent re-derivation.

**Contracts touched**: closes the last concrete item in
`CORE_RUNTIME_CONTRACTS.md`'s "Known migration debt" (§13); is the
first real *consumer* of the already-designed `Deployment.capabilities`/
`Deployment.telemt` shapes (§3.1/§3.2) — everything up to now has only
*modeled* those fields, nothing has *read* them for a real decision
yet.

**What must NOT change**:
- The four topologies' actual generated output (nginx config content,
  Xray inbound JSON) — byte-identical before/after, exactly the
  discipline every prior Candidate in this series has held itself to.
- `cli.sh`'s user-facing prompts/UX.
- `WEB_SERVER`'s handling — deliberately out of scope (open question,
  F5/§14, not to be silently resolved as a side effect of this stage).
- `lifecycle_state`/RECONCILE/health — not touched; this stage is
  Desired-State plumbing only, no Observed-State or reconciliation
  work.
- `lib/core/topology.sh`/`runtime_component.sh` — already correct,
  not to be modified for "consistency" with the new work.

**Necessary tests** (following the established pattern in this repo's
own test suite exactly):
1. Byte-identity: generated `nginx.conf`/Xray-inbounds JSON unchanged
   for all four topologies × XHTTP on/off × TeleMT present/absent,
   before vs after.
2. MODE/positional-blind audit: `F_XHTTP_ENABLE`/`TELEMT_DOMAIN`/
   `TELEMT_PORT` appear as literal strings **only** inside
   `core_resolve_deployment()` after the change (mirrors
   `test_deployment_resolver.sh`'s own MODE-blind assertion style).
3. Negative mutation: corrupt one field of a resolved `Deployment`,
   confirm the specific downstream consumer that should react, does.
4. Explicit-argument-precedence test (same shape as the XHTTP adapter's
   own "explicit 5th arg beats Core accessor" test) if any call site
   needs to keep an explicit override path during transition.
5. `bash -n` / `shellcheck` on every touched file, per repo convention.

**Completion criteria**:
- Zero remaining literal `$11`/`$12`/`$13`/`${13:-0}`-style positional
  reads of these three specific values anywhere outside
  `core_resolve_deployment()`'s own input-collection point.
- All new + all existing test suites green (the `jq`-sandbox caveat
  from §2 aside, which is an environment limitation, not a suite
  outcome).
- No change to any generated artifact's bytes for any existing
  topology/capability combination.

**Why this stage, and not another one**: every other candidate next
step named in this review (F1's SSH timeout/trap work, the Reconciler,
general health/observed-state, RECONCILE/REPAIR/REINSTALL,
`lifecycle_state`, `WEB_SERVER`'s model home) either (a) requires
inventing a genuinely new architecture decision this review found no
settled answer for — which would violate the freeze discipline this
whole exercise exists to protect — or (b) is cross-cutting enough (SSH
lifecycle touches every remote-state call site at once) that it
deserves its own dedicated design pass rather than being bundled
opportunistically. The positional-argument chain is the **only**
remaining item that is simultaneously (1) fully specified already, on
paper, with no new decision required, (2) bounded to a known, small
set of files, (3) directly continues the exact "wire an
already-modeled Core value into one legacy call site, prove
byte-identical output" pattern that has a 100% track record across
Candidates 1, 3, and the two `adapter_reality.sh` functions in this
series, and (4) is explicitly still open per the project's own most
current, most-verified document (`CORE_RUNTIME_CONTRACTS.md` §13).

